# Build context for Dockerfile.controller, Dockerfile.runner and
# Dockerfile.legacy-ansible-runner. All three build from this directory,
# so all three see exactly this filter.
#
# This file denies everything and then allows back only what a build
# genuinely needs. The inverse shape (list what to exclude) was rejected
# deliberately: it fails open, so the next directory added to the
# repository root joins the build context silently and nobody finds out
# until an image is unexpectedly large or contains something it should
# not. Denying by default means a new directory is excluded until someone
# decides otherwise, which is the direction an error should point.
#
# What this was worth, measured before it existed: the context was 410 MB.
# It carried .git, a complete second checkout under .claude/worktrees/,
# roughly 190 MB of stale binaries left in the repository root, and
# .SPECIFICATION/. That last one is why this is a security control and not
# housekeeping. The specification directories are gitignored and never
# ship, and tools/docs-lint runs in `make ci` for the single purpose of
# enforcing that at every surface a user can see. The image build was a
# surface nothing guarded. Multi-stage builds mean those files did not
# reach the final image, so this was never a shipped leak, but it is the
# same control failing at an unguarded boundary, and it becomes a real
# leak the first time a builder stage is cached or pushed.
#
# internal/testsupport's dockerignore_test.go asserts the deny-by-default
# shape, the specific paths that must stay out, and that every allowance
# below is one a Dockerfile actually needs, so a well-meaning "just allow
# one more thing" cannot quietly undo it. That last check is the newest,
# and it is here because this file already had one allowance nothing
# needed: `!tools/`, justified as "the build stage may run repo-local
# tooling". No stage runs any. Neither binary's module graph reaches a
# package under tools/, and no Dockerfile copies from it. An unnecessary
# allowance in a deny-by-default control is the exact shape of the failure
# this file argues against, so it was removed rather than reworded.

# Deny everything.
*

# Allow the module definition. Both Go Dockerfiles copy these first, on
# their own, so that `go mod download` caches in a layer that a source
# edit does not invalidate.
!go.mod
!go.sum

# Allow the source the two Go binaries compile from. cmd/ holds the
# composition roots, and the other two are everything they import.
#
# internal/ is also what puts internal/redact/rules.json in the context,
# which Dockerfile.legacy-ansible-runner copies on its own. Narrowing this
# line to Go sources would break that image while every Go build kept
# working, so dockerignore_test.go names that file as a required input
# rather than leaving it to be rediscovered.
!cmd/
!internal/
!pkg/
